instead of specializing on formal types, you can formalize on traits and make sure various types just conform to one of the traits :)

So say you had 15 types that resulted in a particular body of code and 2 types that used another body of code (meaning we have 17 specializations  here)

The right thing to do here is to have just 2 specializations based off 2 traits and ask these types to conform to either of the above traits. Of course, 
this only works with Methods as structs cannot be specialized on 2 separate traits. No such thing as struct/class overloading.


Here is an e.g., 
template <typename T, typename enable_if<is_good<T>::value, T>::type * = nullptr>
void print(T input) { cout << "good method" << endl; }

template <typename T, typename = typename enable_if<is_bad<T>::value, T>::type>
void print(T input) { cout << "bad method"  << endl; print(input.base);}

Notice the 2 different methods of placing bounds around type T. They are both acceptable btw.


